===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*teh₂g-|id=touch}}
From {{inh|en|enm|taxe}}, from {{der|en|frm|taxe}}, from {{der|en|la-med|taxa}}, from {{cog|la|taxō|t=to appraise, value, estimate; (''medieval'') to tax}}. {{doublet|en|task}}. Displaced native {{ncog|ang|gafol}}, which was also the word for “tribute” and “rent”.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# [[money|Money]] or goods collected by a [[government]] (or an entity to whom the government has delegated this power, e.g. in [[tax farming]]) to fund itself and its services, for example by levying a charge on [[income]], purchases (sales), [[property]] or [[harvest]], other than that money which is collected by the government in exchange for specific [[goods]] (e.g. the purchase of surplus vehicles).
#: {{syn|en|impost|tribute|contribution|duty|toll|rate|assessment|exaction|custom|demand|levy}}
#: {{ant|en|subsidy}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2008|author=Kunzang Choden|title=Chilli and Cheese: Food and Society in Bhutan|page=147
|text=[They paid a] "mushroom '''tax'''" in addition to their regular '''tax''' of butter and meat. They had to give 1 ''dre'' of dried mushrooms annually to the district administrative centres.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2010-12-06|author=Madison Smartt Bell|title=Lavoisier in the Year One: The Birth of a New Science in an Age of Revolution (Great Discoveries)|publisher=W. W. Norton & Company|isbn=9780393341102|page=5
|text=The [General Farm] organization collected a '''tax''' on salt (''gabelle'') and another on alcohol and tobacco (''aide''), along with customs duties (''traites'') and duties on goods entering Paris from elsewhere in France (''entrées''). Evasion of all of these '''taxes''' through smugglign and other fraud was epidemic{{nb...}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-05-17
|author=w:George Monbiot|title=Money just makes the rich suffer
|volume=188|issue=23|page=19
|magazine=w:The Guardian Weekly
|url=http://www.guardian.co.uk/commentisfree/2013/may/06/politics-envy-keenest-rich
|passage=In order to grant the rich these pleasures, the social contract is reconfigured. [&hellip;]&nbsp; Essential public services are cut so that the rich may pay less '''tax'''. The public realm is privatised, the regulations restraining the ultra-wealthy and the companies they control are abandoned, and Edwardian levels of inequality are almost fetishised.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20261011071319/https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2013/may/06/politics-envy-keenest-rich|archivedate=11 October 2026}}
# A [[charge]] (of money, food, labor, etc) collected by a person, organization, etc<!--the "etc" is: an animal-->; something required (exacted) from someone who is (really or notionally) under the control of the [[taxer]], such as a contribution or service.
#* {{RQ:Beaumont Fletcher Comedies and Tragedies|The Queen of Corinth|passage=Flie far from hence <br>All private '''taxes''', immodest phrases, <br>What e'r may but shew like vicious.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2023-11-20|author=Kates Kennery|title=Pet Cook Book First Edition - Dog and Cat fresh food options plus meals|publisher=Kates Kennery
|text=Scrambled egg with cheese [...] careful with the cheese here because it is the main reason for Roxie's runs... [but] We pay a lot of cheese '''tax''' in the house [i.e., when we eat cheese, the dog demands to also be given some].}}
# {{lb|en|figurative|uncountable}} A [[burdensome]] [[demand]]
#: {{ux|en|a heavy '''tax''' on time or health}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1843| title=Accounts and Papers of the House of Commons - Volume 39 |author=| page=234| passage=In the expectation that such would be the case, I came but slightly attended, sending most of my people with the heavy baggage by sea to the Indus, and I took every precaution to render the '''tax''' of my support as light as possible, by furnishing a memorandum of the number of persons composing my suite, and limiting the amount of supplies each should receive.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1962|month=August|author=G. Freeman Allen|title=Traffic control on the Great Northern Line|journal=Modern Railways|page=128|text=The extent of the traffic is a '''tax''' on the existing yard in the area at Frodingham, the busiest in the District.}}

